<body>
<h1>Unit 23: DC Circuits</h1><p class="meta">Physics XI  |  NEB (Grade 11)  |  Phy. 101 &nbsp;•&nbsp; Content area: Electric &amp; Magnetism</p><p class="summary">Circuit analysis is conservation law with a convenient vocabulary: charge conservation becomes Kirchhoff's current law, energy conservation becomes the loop law, and Ohm's law is the constitutive relation that connects them - where it is allowed to apply.</p><h2>What the model asserts</h2><ol><li>Ohm's law V = IR describes ohmic components only; a filament lamp and a diode are non-ohmic, with resistance that changes as current or temperature changes.</li><li>Kirchhoff's current law is charge conservation at a node; the loop law is energy conservation around a closed path.</li><li>Series resistances add and parallel conductances add, so series-parallel reduction is the workhorse method for solvable networks.</li><li>A source with internal resistance r delivers V = E - Ir, so its terminal voltage falls as the current it supplies rises.</li><li>Maximum power transfer to the load occurs when the load resistance equals the internal resistance, but then only half of the generated power reaches the load.</li><li>Joule heating P = I^2 R is why conductors and components get hot, and rated wattage is what limits safe operation.</li><li>Kirchhoff's laws are bookkeeping identities - charge conservation and energy conservation in disguise - so they hold for any lumped circuit regardless of the physics of the individual elements.</li></ol><h2>Conditions, assumptions and limits</h2><ul><li>The lumped-circuit model assumes ideal wires and components that are small compared with the wavelength; at high frequencies transmission lines and distributed effects take over.</li><li>Kirchhoff's laws assume quasi-static behaviour, so no charge accumulates inside the wires and each node has a well-defined potential.</li><li>Ohmic behaviour is temperature-dependent, so treating R as constant is an approximation that matters in power calculations.</li><li>A real battery is not a fixed source: its terminal voltage and internal resistance both depend on state of charge, temperature and current.</li><li>Series and parallel reduction assumes ideal wires and lumped elements; at high frequency the wire's own resistance and inductance take over and the ideal rules stop holding.</li></ul><h2>Exam coverage — 7 NEB topics</h2><ul><li>Electric currents; drift velocity and its relation with current</li><li>Ohm's law; electrical resistance; resistivity; conductivity</li><li>Current-voltage relations; ohmic and non-ohmic resistance</li><li>Resistances in series and parallel</li><li>Potential divider</li><li>Electromotive force of a source, internal resistance</li><li>Work and power in electrical circuits</li></ul><h2>Common misconceptions — 3 to unlearn</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>“Ohm's law V = IR applies to every component, so every resistor has a fixed resistance.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Ohm's law is a statement about a class of materials whose resistance does not change with the applied voltage. Many everyday components are not ohmic.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> For a metal conductor at constant temperature, R is constant and V is proportional to I. A filament lamp is non-ohmic: as the current rises the filament heats, its resistance rises and the V-I graph curves. A diode is strongly non-ohmic and conducts in only one direction.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“The current in a circuit is consumed by the components it passes through.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> Current is the rate of flow of charge and is conserved everywhere in a series circuit; energy is transferred, not charge.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Charge is conserved: the same charge per second passes through every element of a series circuit, which is Kirchhoff's current law. What is 'used up' is energy: the potential energy of the charges falls as they do work against resistance, and that energy becomes heat.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>“A voltmeter connected in series across a component measures the drop across it.”</b></p><p><b>Why it fails:</b> A voltmeter must be connected in parallel, and an ammeter in series. A voltmeter in series has a very large resistance, so it effectively opens the circuit.</p><p><b>Correct:</b> Voltmeters have a very high resistance (ideally infinite) and are connected in parallel with the component; ammeters have a very low resistance and are connected in series. Connecting a voltmeter in series leaves almost no current, and the reading is then the supply voltage rather than a drop across the component.</p></div><h2>Workbook — 3 questions with answers</h2><div class="item"><p class="status">numerical</p><p><b>A cell of emf 12 V and internal resistance 0.50 ohm is connected to a load of 5.5 ohm. Find the current, the terminal voltage of the cell and the power delivered to the load.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> I = E / (R + r) = 12 / (5.5 + 0.5) = 2.0 A. Terminal voltage V = E - I r = 12 - 2.0 x 0.5 = 11 V (and V = I R = 2.0 x 5.5 = 11 V, as it must be). Power in the load P = I V = 2.0 x 11 = 22 W. The power lost inside the cell is I^2 r = 4.0 x 0.5 = 2.0 W, and the total power supplied by the cell is E I = 24 W, so the energy balance is 22 + 2 = 24 W.</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">derivation</p><p><b>Derive Kirchhoff's loop law from the conservation of energy, and show that the sum of the potential changes around any closed loop in a circuit is zero.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> Take any closed loop in a steady circuit. Around the loop, the work done by the electric field in moving a charge q once around must be zero, because the charge returns to its starting point with the same energy: the electrostatic field is conservative, so the work around a closed path vanishes. The work done by the field in moving the charge through each element is q times the potential change across it, so q sum(delta V) = 0 and sum(delta V) = 0. In terms of the circuit elements, the sum of the emf contributions equals the sum of the voltage drops: sum(E) = sum(I R). The same argument applied to a node, where charge is neither created nor destroyed, gives Kirchhoff's current law, sum(I_in) = sum(I_out).</p></div><div class="item"><p class="status">reasoning</p><p><b>Explain, using the V-I characteristic of a filament lamp, why a lamp of rated 100 W, 230 V behaves differently from a resistor when the supply voltage is varied, and what happens if the supply voltage is suddenly increased.</b></p><p><b>Answer:</b> For a metal resistor the resistance is constant, so I is proportional to V and the power rises as V^2. For a filament lamp the current heats the filament, the metal becomes hotter, its resistance rises (the resistivity of a metal increases with temperature), and the V-I curve bends upwards, so the current grows less than proportionally. The rated values 100 W and 230 V give a nominal current of 0.435 A and a resistance of 529 ohms at the working temperature, but the cold resistance is much smaller, which is why a lamp struck from cold draws a large initial current and the filament glows up over a fraction of a second. If the supply voltage is raised, the operating point moves up the curve, the filament runs hotter, its resistance is still higher, and the power - and the temperature, and the rate of evaporation of the filament - rise sharply, which is why overvoltage shortens the life of a lamp.</p></div><h2>Key numbers</h2><table><tr><th>Quantity</th><th>Value</th></tr><tr><td>Electrical power of a 60 W bulb</td><td>0.25 A at 240 V</td></tr><tr><td>Cold resistance of a 100 W, 230 V lamp</td><td>about 529 ohms</td></tr><tr><td>Resistance of 1 m of 1 mm^2 copper wire at 20 degrees C</td><td>about 0.017 ohms</td></tr><tr><td>Power dissipated in a 1 kohm resistor at 10 V</td><td>0.1 W</td></tr></table><h2>Open questions and mysteries</h2><div class="mystery"><p><b>What sets the energy floor for computing with superconducting circuits?</b></p><p class="status">Frontier</p><p>Superconducting qubits and single-photon detectors push towards the energy-time uncertainty limit for readout, which is now the binding constraint in several architectures.</p></div><div class="mystery"><p><b>Can computation be performed with less energy per operation than a transistor switch requires?</b></p><p class="status">Open question</p><p>Landauer sets a thermodynamic floor and the end of Moore's-law scaling has moved the constraint into interconnect and memory energy.</p></div><h2>Inquiry prompts</h2><ol><li>Solve a two-loop circuit using Kirchhoff's laws, then verify the answer by series-parallel reduction.</li><li>Using the internal-resistance model, compare the efficiency for a load matched to the source with one that is not.</li><li>Explain from the electron's point of view why a wire carrying current is hotter than the same wire with no current.</li></ol><h2>Next steps on this platform</h2><ul><li><a href="/class-11-notes/physics/chapters/dc-circuits">Read the chapter notes: DC Circuits</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-calc-ohms">Ohm's law calculator</a></li><li><a href="/lab/ph-th-current">Current electricity theory lab</a></li></ul><h2>Further reading</h2><ul><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/index">OpenStax: University Physics Volume 2</a></li><li><a href="https://www.nist.gov/pml/owm/metric-si/si-units">NIST: SI Units</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-3-resistivity-and-resistance">OpenStax: Resistivity and Resistance</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/9-4-ohms-law">OpenStax: Ohm's Law</a></li><li><a href="https://openstax.org/books/university-physics-volume-2/pages/10-3-kirchhoffs-rules">OpenStax: Kirchhoff's Rules</a></li></ul><p class="note">Physics XI — Academic Companion — a companion brief, not a replacement for the prescribed NEB text. The open questions describe live research frontiers; they are enrichment, not a claim that the established school-level model is invalid. Check the prescribed text and syllabus for examinable scope.</p>
